\section{Dynamical Systems, Chaos, and Attractors}

\subsection{General Dynamical System}

A dynamical system is a pair:


\[
  (X, F),
\]


where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $X$ is a state space (topological, metric, or differentiable),
  \item $F : X \to X$ is an evolution operator.
\end{itemize}

The $n$-step evolution is:


\[
  F^{(n)} = \underbrace{F \circ F \circ \cdots \circ F}_{n \text{ times}}.
\]



Continuous-time systems satisfy:


\[
  \frac{dx}{dt} = f(x),
\]


with flow:


\[
  \varphi_t(x_0).
\]



\subsection{Fixed Points}

A point $x_\ast \in X$ is a fixed point if:


\[
  F(x_\ast) = x_\ast
  \quad \text{or} \quad
  f(x_\ast) = 0.
\]



Stability is determined by the Jacobian:


\[
  J = DF(x_\ast),
\]


or for continuous systems:


\[
  J = Df(x_\ast).
\]



A fixed point is stable if:


\[
  \rho(J) < 1
  \quad \text{(discrete)},
